used  because what he's about to do is  emotionally one of the most difficult  things he's going to do so right there  we got one right that appeared because  you know budget like with these grand  ideas of so many things happening and  then you end up really just getting one  or two of these things and he has I  think just a basic Idol  I think in the end sometimes lack of  budget or time constraints they make you  make a decision you wouldn't normally  make but it's for the better I think  that the focus staying on Kratos really  making this somber moment I'm not making  it too chaotic and not introducing  obviously the hell walkers until much  later worked out really well and see  this this is kind of cool we initially  come in and we have kind of a tray Asst  right here in this space and then we see  the body of Fay right here so at the  beginning of the game you're getting  this composition of the door opening up  and creaking open and seeing at Reyes  stand and look in at the body of Fay and  now we're using sort of a negative space  to show that what we have Kratos coming  in to this much darker scenario it's  like every time you go home not  necessarily the greatest thing at the  beginning of the game we actually show  this point so there's a point where he's  closing the door and we kind of tilt the  camera just for a second and then pull  back up and show that the blades were  there and a few people caught it at one  point it was glowing so it was super  obvious you just see this yellow orange  glow coming out of it and this scene  right here Chris judge I had him do this  like two or three times and I didn't  realize how much it was affecting him I  mean he really went deep on this one and  we had to actually rerecord some of the  lines he was saying because he was  actually crying so much during it that  it was harder to catch some of the bits  of Thailand but it was amazing and I  didn't even realize he actually came to  me after we did the third taking he's  like man can I can I get a break I'm a  little worn out and I was like I'm like  I'm a terrible person yes please we need  to take a break down we were very  worried about how we were gonna get  chains wrapping around the arms so  initially we were shooting around that  so we always have this little trick we  have to pulled it at some point they're  sort of already attached right there and  it's just that sleight of hand you have  to pull that is a rigging actual  Grossman as our our technical rigor we  just use a rope on set and kind of  wrapped it around and then they were  able to kind of make that work  this moment for me is such an incredibly  important moment because in that  beginning pitch that I talked to people  about I kept talking about this idea  that it's not like we're gonna erase  Kratos his past it's not like all of a  sudden you started this game and credits  great guy and he's going to saving  people and he's gonna be a good dad  no he's the same guy right it's just  that in the previous games the monster  that was inside of him was let out all  the time and I said in this one he's  making the attempts to actually keep the  monster inside he's struggling and he's  failing constantly but this moment of  accepting that he's a monster right is I  think the crux of everything we did in  this story it was very important that at  that end point he was standing up and  accepting who he is and then he would  just walk straight through Athena  dissipating her and you would be  wandering the entire time was she real  was she in his head or was she real and  in his head I think both of those things  are entirely possible  let's see this is my favorite moment to  watch on the streets I really like  watching the moments that you know  emotionally will get to people you had  gone through all of this wondering  whirring maybe that's not what they're  gonna do I could be reading into this  too much oh my gosh that's what they're  gonna do this is gonna be 